		<title>Adobe is testing how well AI agents can use editing tools like a Photoshop professional would</title>
		<link>https://the-decoder.com/adobe-is-testing-how-well-ai-agents-can-use-editing-tools-like-a-photoshop-professional-would/</link>
					<comments>https://the-decoder.com/adobe-is-testing-how-well-ai-agents-can-use-editing-tools-like-a-photoshop-professional-would/#respond</comments>
		
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Jonathan Kemper]]></dc:creator>
		<pubDate>Sun, 01 Nov 2026 14:56:44 +0000</pubDate>
				<category><![CDATA[AI research]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Artificial Intelligence]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Adobe]]></category>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://the-decoder.com/?p=42278</guid>

					<description><![CDATA[<p><img width="2048" height="1152" src="https://the-decoder.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/adobe_logo-2.png" class="attachment-full size-full wp-post-image" alt="" style="height: auto; margin-bottom: 10px;" decoding="async" /></p>
<p>        Adobe Research and UC San Diego built EditAgent, an AI system that edits images more like a Photoshop pro. Instead of retrying the same general-purpose editor over and over, the agent picks the right specialized tool for each task and lets a standard language model choose the best result based on measured image data. The approach works, but it needs about ten times the compute.</p>
<p>The article <a href="https://the-decoder.com/adobe-is-testing-how-well-ai-agents-can-use-editing-tools-like-a-photoshop-professional-would/">Adobe is testing how well AI agents can use editing tools like a Photoshop professional would</a> appeared first on <a href="https://the-decoder.com">The Decoder</a>.</p>
]]></description>
		
