		<category><![CDATA[Artificial Intelligence]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Google]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Google Deepmind]]></category>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://the-decoder.com/?p=42254</guid>

					<description><![CDATA[<p><img width="1920" height="1080" src="https://the-decoder.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/02-streamvibe-kochassistent-1920x1080-1.jpg" class="attachment-full size-full wp-post-image" alt="" style="height: auto; margin-bottom: 10px;" decoding="async" /></p>
<p>        A Google-led research team built StreamVibe, a video agent designed to watch people cook or assemble furniture and speak up only when help is needed. A benchmark shows that none of the tested models reliably pick the right moment in longer videos, as errors compound and response quality drops sharply.</p>
<p>The article <a href="https://the-decoder.com/google-wants-video-ai-to-understand-what-people-need-as-they-workgoogle-researchers-are-teaching-a-video-assistant-when-to-offer-help/">Google wants video AI to understand what people need as they work</a> appeared first on <a href="https://the-decoder.com">The Decoder</a>.</p>
]]></description>
		
